{
  "fromId": "f",
  "toId": "glottal_stop",
  "preamble": "Debuccalization of [f] all the way to a glottal stop [ʔ] is a regular sound law in part of Eastern Polynesian. In Rarotongan and Mangaian (Cook Islands Māori), Proto-Polynesian *f became ʔ (written ‘ or ʻ): *fale > ‘are 'house', *fenua > ‘enua 'land', *fetuʔu > ‘ētū 'star', *fafine > va‘ine 'woman'. Tuamotuan shows the same outcome word-internally in va‘ine, while related languages keep [f] or have [h] (Tahitian fare, vahine; Māori whare, wahine).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Debuccalization, Glottalization, Lenition",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Rarotongan (Cook Islands Māori)",
      "languageFamily": "Polynesian",
